aligned() rounds a pointer up by casting it to uintptr_t and back, which on a
capability target yields an address without a tag, and the test faults on its
own first access. Adding the rounding distance to the original pointer keeps
its provenance and reaches the same address. Same helper in three tests.

diff --git i/src/functional/string_memcpy.c w/src/functional/string_memcpy.c
index 96256a7..264606d 100644
--- i/src/functional/string_memcpy.c
+++ w/src/functional/string_memcpy.c
@@ -8,7 +8,7 @@ static char buf[512];
 static void *(*volatile pmemcpy)(void *restrict, const void *restrict, size_t);
 
 static void *aligned(void *p) {
-	return (void*)(((uintptr_t)p + 63) & -64);
+	return (char*)p + ((-(uintptr_t)p) & 63);
 }
 
 #define N 80
